(window.DOM_TESTS=window.DOM_TESTS||{})["circulation-3"]={"lecture":"circulation-3","chapters":["gravity","shock","heart-failure"],"questions":[{"id":"lt-circulation-3:cddff8f2","chapter":"gravity","section":"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above","type":"calculation","stem":"A subject stands upright without compensatory changes. The brain is 40 cm above heart level. By approximately how much does gravity lower arterial pressure at the brain?","options":{"A":"15 mmHg lower than at heart level.","B":"31 mmHg lower than at heart level.","C":"46 mmHg lower than at heart level.","D":"77 mmHg lower than at heart level."},"answer":"B","why":"Blood produces a hydrostatic gradient of about 0.77 mmHg per centimetre. A 40-cm upward difference therefore subtracts approximately 40 × 0.77 = 30.8 mmHg.","not":{"A":"This corresponds to about 0.38 mmHg/cm, which is not the stated gradient.","C":"This assumes a gradient of about 1.15 mmHg/cm rather than 0.77.","D":"This is the pressure difference for about 100 cm, not 40 cm."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:56faa67e","chapter":"gravity","section":"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A standing subject has mean pressure of about 93 mmHg at heart level. Which head-to-feet pressure pattern is expected without compensation?","options":{"A":"Head near 180 mmHg and feet near 60 mmHg.","B":"Head near 120 mmHg and feet near 80 mmHg.","C":"Head near 93 mmHg and feet near 93 mmHg.","D":"Head near 60 mmHg and feet near 180 mmHg."},"answer":"D","why":"The hydrostatic blood column lowers pressure above the heart and raises it below. The hydrostatic illustration corresponds to roughly 60 mmHg at head level and 180 mmHg at the feet.","not":{"A":"The stated pattern reverses the effect of height relative to the heart.","B":"Those are systolic and diastolic heart-level values, not head and foot mean pressures.","C":"Equal pressures would ignore the hydrostatic gradient created by standing."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:a2143b94","chapter":"gravity","section":"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can venous pressure in a standing person’s feet be high even when the right atrial pressure is low?","options":{"A":"Leg veins generate pressure by actively contracting.","B":"The right ventricle directly raises pressure in leg veins.","C":"Arterial valves transmit systolic pressure into leg veins.","D":"The blood column adds pressure to dependent veins."},"answer":"D","why":"Hydrostatic pressure increases below the heart because a tall column of blood has weight. The pressure rise does not require high central venous pressure or venous contraction.","not":{"A":"Veins are compliant capacitance vessels rather than active pressure-generating pumps.","B":"The right ventricle is separated from systemic leg veins by the pulmonary circulation.","C":"Gravity creates the pressure gradient; direct arterial-valve transmission does not explain it."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e2ae0f30","chapter":"gravity","section":"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation","type":"mechanism","stem":"Immediately after a student stands up, a substantial amount of blood collects in the legs. What property makes this pooling possible?","options":{"A":"The high resistance of capillaries prevents venous emptying.","B":"The strong contraction of venous walls traps incoming blood.","C":"The high compliance of peripheral veins allows them to expand.","D":"The reduced compliance of arteries redirects blood into veins."},"answer":"C","why":"Peripheral veins are capacitance vessels that distend when hydrostatic pressure rises. They can temporarily hold several hundred millilitres, reducing return to the central circulation.","not":{"A":"Capillary resistance alone does not explain the main pooling reservoir.","B":"Strong venous contraction would tend to empty rather than expand the reservoir.","D":"A change in arterial compliance is not the mechanism given for dependent venous pooling."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e76b480f","chapter":"gravity","section":"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation","type":"application","stem":"A person stands motionless and develops mild ankle swelling after some time. What initial change most directly promotes this fluid accumulation?","options":{"A":"Lymphatic valves reverse the direction of fluid movement.","B":"Plasma oncotic pressure rises sharply in dependent tissues.","C":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure increases below heart level.","D":"Arteriolar pressure falls to the level of venous pressure."},"answer":"C","why":"Gravity increases pressure in dependent vessels, including capillaries. The higher capillary hydrostatic pressure favours filtration of water from blood into the interstitial compartment.","not":{"A":"The problem begins with increased filtration, not reversed lymphatic valve function.","B":"Higher plasma oncotic pressure would oppose rather than promote filtration.","D":"A general fall in arteriolar pressure does not account for the dependent filtration increase."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:414a444d","chapter":"gravity","section":"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation","type":"causal-sequence","stem":"After sudden standing, venous return falls before reflex compensation begins. Which sequence best explains the transient decrease in cardiac output?","options":{"A":"Lower end-diastolic volume reduces stroke volume by Frank–Starling.","B":"Higher peripheral resistance directly increases ventricular filling volume.","C":"Higher end-diastolic volume reduces stroke volume by Frank–Starling.","D":"Lower end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume by Frank–Starling."},"answer":"A","why":"Pooling lowers venous return and ventricular end-diastolic volume. The ventricle starts with less filling, so Frank–Starling predicts a smaller stroke volume and consequently lower cardiac output.","not":{"B":"Peripheral resistance affects pressure and afterload but does not replace the lost venous return.","C":"A rise in ventricular filling is not the immediate result of peripheral pooling.","D":"Less preload on the normal ventricular function curve reduces rather than increases stroke volume."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:539cc65e","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"In a healthy person, arterial baroreceptors are less stretched just after standing. Which neural change should follow?","options":{"A":"Both sympathetic and cardiac vagal activity increase together.","B":"Sympathetic activity rises while cardiac vagal activity falls.","C":"Both autonomic outputs decrease until the pressure recovers.","D":"Sympathetic activity falls while cardiac vagal activity rises."},"answer":"B","why":"Less baroreceptor stretch reduces afferent firing to the medulla. The baroreflex releases sympathetic drive and reduces vagal braking to help restore vascular tone and cardiac output.","not":{"A":"Simultaneous strong vagal activation would oppose the necessary tachycardia.","C":"Reduced sympathetic drive would impair immediate pressure compensation.","D":"This is the usual autonomic response to an increase, not a decrease, in pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:25a50d1b","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which response helps restore venous return in the first seconds after the onset of standing?","options":{"A":"Sympathetic venoconstriction mobilises blood stored in veins.","B":"Parasympathetic venodilation increases venous emptying into the heart.","C":"Renal erythropoietin release immediately expands circulating volume.","D":"Arteriolar dilation pulls blood out of veins into capillaries."},"answer":"A","why":"Increased sympathetic tone contracts compliant veins, reduces their storage capacity, and shifts blood towards the central veins and heart, supporting ventricular filling.","not":{"B":"Venodilation tends to increase peripheral storage rather than return.","C":"Erythropoietin acts on red-cell production over days to weeks, not seconds.","D":"General arteriolar dilation would lower resistance and further threaten arterial pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:34f550a0","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"A student says that the increased chronotropic and inotropic responses to standing are interchangeable. Which distinction is correct?","options":{"A":"Chronotropy raises stroke volume; inotropy raises blood volume.","B":"Chronotropy raises venous tone; inotropy raises heart rate.","C":"Chronotropy raises force; inotropy raises vascular resistance.","D":"Chronotropy raises rate; inotropy raises force of contraction."},"answer":"D","why":"The baroreflex increases heart rate through a positive chronotropic effect and myocardial contractile strength through a positive inotropic effect. Both support cardiac output by different mechanisms.","not":{"A":"Neither term directly describes total blood volume, which changes on a slower timescale.","B":"Venous tone is a vascular response, not the meaning of chronotropy.","C":"Contractile force is inotropy, while vascular resistance is a separate effect."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:4c059782","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why may cerebral perfusion be better preserved during standing than the fall in head-level arterial pressure alone suggests?","options":{"A":"Heart-level arterial pressure is transmitted unchanged to the brain.","B":"Cerebral venous pressure rises and pushes blood forward.","C":"Intracranial pressure rises and opens cerebral arterioles.","D":"Cerebral venous and intracranial pressures also decrease."},"answer":"D","why":"Perfusion depends on arterial pressure relative to downstream venous or intracranial pressure. When both downstream pressures fall with standing, the effective gradient decreases less than arterial pressure alone.","not":{"A":"Hydrostatic height means arterial pressure at the head is not identical to heart-level pressure.","B":"Rising venous back-pressure would impede, not support, cerebral perfusion.","C":"Higher intracranial pressure increases external compression on cerebral vessels."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:61119538","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"If cerebral blood flow decreases slightly on standing, what local response in brain vessels limits the reduction?","options":{"A":"Rising tissue CO₂ favours cerebral arteriolar dilation.","B":"Falling tissue CO₂ favours cerebral arteriolar dilation.","C":"Falling tissue O₂ causes a primary cerebral venoconstriction.","D":"Rising tissue CO₂ favours cerebral arteriolar constriction."},"answer":"A","why":"Reduced washout permits CO₂ to increase locally; cerebral arterioles dilate and lower resistance, helping preserve flow despite the hydrostatic challenge.","not":{"B":"A fall in CO₂ generally opposes cerebral dilation rather than producing it.","C":"The protective local change concerns arteriolar resistance, not venous constriction.","D":"CO₂ acts as a potent cerebral vasodilatory signal in this setting."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:c32da2c8","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"application","stem":"A guard has stood motionless for a long time and becomes faint. Which missing physiological action most strongly contributes to the event?","options":{"A":"Leg-muscle contraction that normally squeezes blood towards the heart.","B":"Pulmonary arterial constriction that normally boosts cerebral perfusion.","C":"Renal sodium excretion that normally expands blood volume rapidly.","D":"Cardiac contraction that normally raises cerebral venous pressure."},"answer":"A","why":"When the legs remain still, the skeletal-muscle pump cannot intermittently compress deep veins. Pooling persists, venous return and cardiac output fall, and cerebral perfusion may become inadequate.","not":{"B":"Pulmonary arteriolar constriction is not the compensatory leg-muscle pump.","C":"Sodium excretion would reduce rather than expand circulating volume.","D":"Increasing cerebral venous pressure would decrease the perfusion gradient."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b2929e96","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"application","stem":"A person loses consciousness after prolonged upright standing but rapidly recovers when lying flat. What most directly explains the recovery?","options":{"A":"Increasing the vertical heart-to-brain pressure difference increases flow.","B":"Raising venous pressure in the head closes cerebral arterioles.","C":"A horizontal position reduces the heart-to-brain pressure gap.","D":"Reducing plasma albumin concentration reverses gravitational pooling."},"answer":"C","why":"In a horizontal position, the brain is close to heart level. The hydrostatic disadvantage to head-level arterial pressure largely disappears, restoring cerebral perfusion.","not":{"A":"A larger vertical distance would worsen the hydrostatic pressure loss at the head.","B":"Greater venous back-pressure would not open the cerebral circulation.","D":"Albumin changes are not responsible for the immediate recovery after fainting."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:888e795c","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Following a frightening experience, a subject faints with bradycardia and vasodilation. How does this differ from the usual immediate reflex to standing?","options":{"A":"It reflects raised sympathetic drive to veins and arterioles.","B":"It reflects isolated cerebral vasoconstriction with stable pressure.","C":"It reflects vagal activation and sympathetic withdrawal.","D":"It reflects vagal withdrawal and sympathetic activation."},"answer":"C","why":"Vasovagal syncope can produce a slow heart and suddenly dilated vessels. Ordinary standing instead normally unloads baroreceptors, causing reflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction.","not":{"A":"Enhanced sympathetic vascular tone would oppose the sudden hypotension.","B":"The faint is associated with systemic hypotension, not isolated local cerebral narrowing.","D":"That is the usual pressure-defending baroreflex response to standing."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:fa97788c","chapter":"gravity","section":"5-orthostatic-postural-hypotension","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Three minutes after standing, a patient’s pressure has changed from 126/78 to 104/74 mmHg. How is this best classified using the standard postural criterion?","options":{"A":"Orthostatic hypotension, because diastolic pressure fell by 22 mmHg.","B":"Orthostatic hypotension, because systolic pressure fell by 22 mmHg.","C":"No orthostatic hypotension, because diastolic pressure fell by 4 mmHg.","D":"No orthostatic hypotension, because the final systolic pressure exceeds 100."},"answer":"B","why":"The systolic pressure decreased by 22 mmHg. A sustained decrease of at least 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within three minutes meets the stated postural criterion.","not":{"A":"The diastolic reading fell only 4 mmHg, not 22 mmHg.","C":"The criterion uses either the systolic or diastolic threshold, not necessarily both.","D":"The criterion depends on the change from supine, not whether the absolute systolic value is above 100."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:71161071","chapter":"gravity","section":"5-orthostatic-postural-hypotension","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A patient with diabetic autonomic neuropathy becomes hypotensive when standing but shows little heart-rate rise. What mechanism best explains this combination?","options":{"A":"Intact baroreflex output produces excessive venous constriction.","B":"Damaged neural pathways blunt reflex sympathetic responses.","C":"Excess aldosterone immediately blocks carotid baroreceptor firing.","D":"Increased vagal withdrawal prevents the normal rise in heart rate."},"answer":"B","why":"Diabetic autonomic neuropathy can damage reflex sympathetic pathways. The pressure drop is detected, but compensatory tachycardia and vasoconstriction are inadequate.","not":{"A":"Excess venoconstriction would usually help prevent the fall in pressure.","C":"Aldosterone does not cause the rapid autonomic failure described.","D":"Vagal withdrawal normally permits heart rate to rise rather than preventing it."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1c833745","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"mechanism","stem":"During sustained strong positive head-to-foot acceleration in an aircraft, which circulation is most threatened first?","options":{"A":"Pulmonary perfusion, because plasma oncotic pressure suddenly rises.","B":"Head-level perfusion, because blood is displaced towards the feet.","C":"Coronary perfusion, because cerebral venous pressure steadily rises.","D":"Foot perfusion, because blood is displaced towards the head."},"answer":"B","why":"Positive g moves blood downwards, increasing pooling in dependent vessels and lowering arterial pressure at the head. Vision and cerebral perfusion can fail when compensation is exceeded.","not":{"A":"Oncotic pressure is not the immediate mechanism of positive-g hypoperfusion.","C":"Positive g lowers rather than consistently raises head-level venous pressure.","D":"Upward blood movement describes negative, not positive, acceleration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:4a163be3","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"application","stem":"A pilot first reports greyed vision during increasing positive g, before losing consciousness. Why is visual failure an early warning?","options":{"A":"Pulmonary capillary filtration directly shuts down retinal signalling.","B":"Retinal venous pressure rises because blood is displaced to the head.","C":"Head-level arterial pressure becomes too low for the retina.","D":"The retina has no direct arterial supply at high acceleration."},"answer":"C","why":"Retinal blood flow depends on arterial pressure at head level and must also overcome intraocular pressure. A fall in that pressure can impair vision before global cerebral perfusion fails.","not":{"A":"The primary immediate cause is hydrostatic loss of retinal perfusion pressure.","B":"Headward blood displacement corresponds to negative g and red-out.","D":"The retina does have arterial blood supply; its perfusion pressure becomes inadequate."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:43be8b68","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"mechanism","stem":"A pilot experiences marked head and neck congestion under negative g, yet intracranial vessels may avoid rupture. What protective change is described?","options":{"A":"Intracranial pressure increases alongside vascular pressure.","B":"Intracranial pressure falls while arterial pressure rises.","C":"Arterial and venous pressures in the head both fall markedly.","D":"Ventricular ejection ceases before venous pressure can increase."},"answer":"A","why":"Blood moves towards the head under negative g. Cerebral venous congestion raises intracranial pressure as intravascular pressure rises, limiting the change in transmural pressure across vessel walls.","not":{"B":"Lower outside pressure would increase vessel wall stress, not protect it.","C":"Negative g causes headward blood movement and congestion, not lowered head pressures.","D":"Rising pressure outside the vessels limits the increase in transmural pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:35a60e07","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"application","stem":"Why do anti-g suits squeeze the legs and abdomen during high positive acceleration?","options":{"A":"They raise intracranial pressure to prevent retinal perfusion.","B":"They increase dependent pooling to store more blood safely.","C":"They eliminate the need for cardiac output during acceleration.","D":"They reduce dependent pooling and support venous return."},"answer":"D","why":"External compression limits expansion of compliant leg and abdominal veins. More blood remains available for central circulation, helping maintain cardiac filling and head perfusion.","not":{"A":"Positive-g protection primarily requires maintaining arterial supply to the head.","B":"More pooling would worsen the very problem the suit is designed to limit.","C":"The heart still needs to pump blood during acceleration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:80e2a542","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Why can the body tolerate a greater acceleration when lying horizontally rather than upright?","options":{"A":"The total amount of circulating blood becomes substantially larger.","B":"The vertical blood column between the heart and brain is shorter.","C":"The heart develops a new pressure-independent pumping mechanism.","D":"The veins cease responding to sympathetic stimulation altogether."},"answer":"B","why":"When acceleration acts across rather than along the body, the effective heart-to-brain column is shorter and therefore creates a smaller hydrostatic pressure difference.","not":{"A":"Posture changes the pressure gradient, not the total blood volume directly.","C":"Cardiac performance still depends on adequate preload and pressure.","D":"Sympathetic venous responses do not vanish because the body is horizontal."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5aa37392","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"A patient has cold extremities, reduced urine output and poor organ perfusion, but mean arterial pressure is near normal. Which explanation is most defensible?","options":{"A":"Normal arterial pressure proves that tissue blood flow is sufficient.","B":"Normal capillary filtration excludes impaired organ circulation.","C":"High peripheral resistance can partly mask a fall in cardiac output.","D":"High cardiac output can only occur when resistance is also high."},"answer":"C","why":"Mean arterial pressure depends on cardiac output and total peripheral resistance. Reflex arteriolar constriction may defend pressure despite a marked fall in output and regional tissue perfusion.","not":{"A":"Shock is defined by inadequate tissue perfusion rather than an isolated pressure number.","B":"Capillary exchange alone cannot establish adequate oxygen delivery or organ flow.","D":"The relationship MAP ≈ CO × TPR does not impose that restriction."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1cd13667","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"classification","stem":"An injured patient has normal circulating volume but severe impairment of ventricular pumping. Which category best identifies the primary disturbance?","options":{"A":"Cardiogenic shock due to pump failure.","B":"Distributive shock due to low resistance.","C":"Hypovolaemic shock due to fluid loss.","D":"Obstructive shock due to blocked outflow."},"answer":"A","why":"Cardiogenic shock results when the heart cannot provide sufficient output despite blood being available. Causes include myocardial infarction, heart failure and serious arrhythmias.","not":{"B":"The problem specified is a failing pump, not widespread vascular dilation.","C":"The stem states that circulating volume is not the primary deficit.","D":"An anatomical obstruction is not described in this presentation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:17ddfee2","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"classification","stem":"A patient has severe tissue hypoperfusion with extensive arteriolar and venous dilation but without an initial loss of total blood volume. What is the main circulatory defect?","options":{"A":"Insufficient blood volume owing to haemorrhage.","B":"Mechanical blockage to the pulmonary circulation.","C":"Primary loss of myocardial contractile force.","D":"Low resistance and redistributed blood flow."},"answer":"D","why":"Distributive shock is described as a low-resistance state. Widespread vasodilation causes vascular pooling and abnormal distribution even when initial total blood volume is preserved.","not":{"A":"Blood loss defines hypovolaemia but is specifically not present.","B":"A physical block would indicate obstructive shock.","C":"Myocardial pump failure defines cardiogenic rather than distributive shock."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:10f26751","chapter":"shock","section":"2-haemorrhage-the-model-for-all-shock","type":"causal-sequence","stem":"Immediately after major haemorrhage, which event lies between loss of blood volume and a fall in stroke volume?","options":{"A":"Increased aortic compliance with unchanged preload.","B":"Reduced venous return and reduced ventricular filling.","C":"Increased venous return and increased ventricular filling.","D":"Reduced myocardial oxygen consumption with greater preload."},"answer":"B","why":"Haemorrhage reduces venous blood available to return to the heart. Lower ventricular end-diastolic filling decreases stroke volume by the Frank–Starling relationship.","not":{"A":"Aortic compliance is not the immediate link between reduced blood volume and stroke volume.","C":"Losing blood does not increase central venous filling.","D":"A change in oxygen use does not replace the fall in preload."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5f3f7911","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why may the pulse feel fast but weak early in haemorrhagic shock?","options":{"A":"An increased stroke volume accompanies enhanced vagal tone.","B":"A reduced heart rate accompanies increased ventricular filling.","C":"Baroreflex tachycardia accompanies a reduced stroke volume.","D":"A slower sinus rate accompanies widespread venous dilation."},"answer":"C","why":"Lower arterial pressure unloads baroreceptors, causing reflex tachycardia. Simultaneously, reduced ventricular filling makes each ejection small, so the pulse is weak.","not":{"A":"Vagal enhancement slows heart rate; a larger stroke volume would not explain a weak pulse.","B":"Haemorrhage reduces filling and usually produces tachycardia initially.","D":"This combines responses opposite to the initial baroreflex compensation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b61c7e5b","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A severely bleeding patient has cold, pale skin while remaining awake. What accounts most directly for the skin finding?","options":{"A":"Local skin metabolites increase perfusion throughout haemorrhage.","B":"Unchanged skin resistance preserves flow despite lower output.","C":"Parasympathetic dilation of skin vessels increases heat loss.","D":"Skin vessels constrict, redirecting flow to vital organs."},"answer":"D","why":"In shock, sympathetic arteriolar constriction is especially strong in the skin and viscera. It helps defend arterial pressure and preferentially protect heart and brain circulation.","not":{"A":"Metabolic dilation does not override the strong early general sympathetic response here.","B":"If skin flow remained unchanged, profound pallor and cooling would be less expected.","C":"Dilation would more often produce warm skin rather than the described pallor."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5b7b5a16","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with haemorrhage produces very little urine despite no urinary tract obstruction. What primarily explains this early finding?","options":{"A":"Reduced renal flow and filtration limit urine production.","B":"Increased glomerular filtration raises water loss through urine.","C":"Increased renal perfusion pressure prevents tubular reabsorption.","D":"Increased renal blood flow stimulates excessive sodium retention."},"answer":"A","why":"Low renal perfusion and sympathetic vasoconstriction reduce glomerular filtration. Concurrent hormonal retention of sodium and water further lowers urine output.","not":{"B":"An increase in filtration would not explain marked oliguria.","C":"Perfusion pressure is compromised, and salt/water conservation is enhanced.","D":"Renal blood flow is typically restricted, not increased, in this response."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:ccca0f09","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"As shock progresses, arterial oxygen delivery becomes inadequate. Which metabolic sequence is expected in poorly perfused tissues?","options":{"A":"More aerobic glycolysis leads to a rapid fall in lactate.","B":"More oxygen extraction stops acid production despite absent flow.","C":"More mitochondrial oxidation produces sustained tissue alkalosis.","D":"More anaerobic glycolysis leads to accumulation of lactic acid."},"answer":"D","why":"With insufficient oxygen delivery, cells increasingly rely on anaerobic glycolysis. Lactate and hydrogen ions accumulate, contributing to metabolic acidosis and impaired tissue function.","not":{"A":"Aerobic metabolism requires adequate oxygen and does not generate the described acid burden.","B":"Extraction cannot compensate indefinitely when oxygen delivery is critically inadequate.","C":"Tissue hypoxia impairs oxidative ATP production rather than enhancing it."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:229bea8a","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"clinical-application","stem":"In a patient with prolonged severe shock, why can lactic acidosis accelerate the circulatory deterioration?","options":{"A":"It protects cerebral vasomotor output from prolonged ischaemia.","B":"It weakens the heart and vascular catecholamine responses.","C":"It strengthens cardiac contraction and heightens vascular tone.","D":"It directly increases blood volume by producing new red cells."},"answer":"B","why":"Severe lactic acidosis depresses myocardial function and reduces peripheral responsiveness to catecholamines. These changes undermine the pressure-defending responses.","not":{"A":"Prolonged brain ischaemia can depress the vasomotor centre rather than protect it.","C":"The described effects of acidosis are detrimental rather than stimulatory.","D":"New red-cell production takes weeks and is not caused by immediate acidosis."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:0fa6a921","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is rapid breathing a common early finding in severe circulatory shock?","options":{"A":"Chemoreceptors and acidosis increase respiratory drive.","B":"Baroreceptor stimulation prevents responses to low oxygen delivery.","C":"Higher plasma oncotic pressure directly accelerates breathing.","D":"Reduced respiratory drive preserves oxygen stores in the tissues."},"answer":"A","why":"Poor oxygen delivery and rising acid levels stimulate ventilatory responses. Chemoreceptor activation contributes to rapid respiration and circulatory compensation.","not":{"B":"Baroreceptors are unloaded by low pressure; they do not eliminate hypoxic drive.","C":"Oncotic pressure is not the direct primary stimulus for breathing here.","D":"Reducing ventilation would not correct the associated oxygen and acid-base disturbances."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:c6a25100","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"clinical-application","stem":"An initially restless patient with major blood loss later becomes dull and apathetic. Which development best explains this change?","options":{"A":"Restored cerebral perfusion reverses sympathetic arousal.","B":"Enhanced skeletal-muscle pumping produces cerebral congestion.","C":"Worsening cerebral ischaemia impairs brain function.","D":"Recovery of plasma proteins suppresses cortical activity."},"answer":"C","why":"Early apprehension reflects sympathetic arousal; later apathy suggests reduced brain perfusion. Progressive cerebral ischaemia is an ominous sign of shock.","not":{"A":"Improved cerebral perfusion should favour alertness rather than apathy.","B":"The muscle pump ordinarily helps venous return rather than causing brain hypoperfusion.","D":"Plasma-protein recovery is not the explanation for an abrupt mental-status decline."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:69d8ebbb","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient in shock reports intense thirst soon after blood loss. Which signals are particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Angiotensin II and vasopressin promote water retention.","B":"Decreased vasopressin and inhibited sodium retention.","C":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide with reduced thirst.","D":"Decreased angiotensin II and increased renal water excretion."},"answer":"A","why":"Hypoperfusion activates renin–angiotensin signalling and vasopressin release. These conserve salt and water and are associated with thirst during volume depletion.","not":{"B":"Vasopressin normally rises to conserve water in this situation.","C":"ANP is not the principal volume-restoring response to haemorrhagic shock.","D":"Low angiotensin II and greater water excretion would worsen hypovolaemia."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:94daf2cb","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can constriction of the splanchnic veins be helpful immediately after severe blood loss?","options":{"A":"It directly increases the total number of circulating red cells.","B":"It retains more blood within a highly compliant abdominal reservoir.","C":"It eliminates the need to maintain peripheral arterial pressure.","D":"It mobilises stored venous blood towards the central circulation."},"answer":"D","why":"The splanchnic veins store a substantial amount of blood. Sympathetic venoconstriction reduces that capacity and helps move blood back to the heart.","not":{"A":"Mobilising existing blood is not the same as producing new red cells.","B":"Greater splanchnic storage would reduce rather than support venous return.","C":"Venous mobilisation assists, but does not replace, other pressure-defending responses."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e6c32106","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Acute blood loss activates the adrenal medulla. What is the immediate benefit to the circulation?","options":{"A":"Circulating natriuretic peptide increases sodium excretion.","B":"Circulating albumin immediately restores plasma oncotic pressure.","C":"Circulating erythropoietin immediately restores haemoglobin concentration.","D":"Circulating catecholamines reinforce sympathetic cardiovascular responses."},"answer":"D","why":"Adrenal medullary catecholamines increase sympathetic effects on the heart and vessels, complementing neural compensation for falling arterial pressure.","not":{"A":"Enhanced natriuresis is not the main acute protective response to blood loss.","B":"The adrenal medulla releases catecholamines, not plasma albumin.","C":"Erythropoietin affects red-cell production on a much longer timescale."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:9029d6fc","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Hours after haemorrhage, fluid begins moving from the interstitial space into capillaries. Which haemodynamic change favours this refill?","options":{"A":"Higher capillary hydrostatic pressure increases outward filtration.","B":"Higher interstitial oncotic pressure favours capillary refill.","C":"Lower capillary pressure reduces outward fluid movement.","D":"Lower plasma oncotic pressure strengthens fluid reabsorption."},"answer":"C","why":"As pressure falls and upstream vessels constrict, capillary hydrostatic pressure decreases. In the classic Starling account this favours movement of tissue fluid into the vascular compartment.","not":{"A":"Raising capillary hydrostatic pressure would drive more fluid into tissues.","B":"A stronger interstitial protein pull favours fluid remaining outside capillaries.","D":"Lower plasma protein pull generally opposes inward fluid movement."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1b23dbae","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which endocrine sequence best supports sodium and water retention after major haemorrhage?","options":{"A":"Renin activation leads to angiotensin II and aldosterone release.","B":"Inhibited angiotensin II production leads to reduced thirst.","C":"Atrial natriuretic peptide release leads to increased sodium loss.","D":"Reduced vasopressin release leads to enhanced urinary water loss."},"answer":"A","why":"Reduced renal perfusion activates renin, leading to angiotensin II and aldosterone. These help defend arterial pressure through vasoconstriction and sodium/water conservation.","not":{"B":"Angiotensin II supports rather than suppresses the thirst and retention response.","C":"ANP favours natriuresis and is not the principal volume-defending pathway here.","D":"Vasopressin rises rather than falls in hypovolaemia."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6a0f03d2","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A healthy person loses a substantial amount of whole blood very recently. Before significant fluid shifts occur, how may haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit appear?","options":{"A":"Near zero, because haemorrhage abruptly stops erythropoiesis.","B":"Initially unchanged, as red cells and plasma are lost.","C":"Markedly reduced, because only red cells were lost at first.","D":"Markedly elevated, because only plasma was lost at first."},"answer":"B","why":"Whole-blood loss removes plasma and red cells together. Initially their ratio in the remaining circulation may stay similar; concentrations fall later as tissue or infused fluid dilutes the blood.","not":{"A":"Stopping red-cell production would not immediately erase circulating haemoglobin.","C":"Bleeding removes whole blood, not red cells alone.","D":"Selective plasma loss describes burns rather than immediate whole-blood haemorrhage."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:a9a18adb","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can measured haematocrit decrease some hours after bleeding even when no more red cells have been lost?","options":{"A":"Aldosterone directly destroys erythrocytes throughout the circulation.","B":"The spleen removes plasma and increases the red-cell concentration.","C":"Tissue fluid entering the circulation dilutes erythrocytes.","D":"Venoconstriction increases the red-cell count per unit of plasma."},"answer":"C","why":"Capillary refill and salt/water retention replace plasma volume faster than red cells. The remaining erythrocytes are diluted, lowering haematocrit after the initial bleed.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone promotes sodium retention and does not directly lyse red cells.","B":"Removal of plasma would tend to concentrate, not dilute, red cells.","D":"Venoconstriction redistributes blood but does not explain delayed dilution."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e5c0cb24","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"time-course","stem":"After bleeding has stopped, plasma volume is restored more quickly than red-cell mass. Which recovery time-course is expected?","options":{"A":"Both volume and haemoglobin recover fully within the first hour.","B":"Volume returns in 12–72 hours; haemoglobin takes several weeks.","C":"Haemoglobin recovers before fluid shifts restore circulating volume.","D":"Volume returns after 4–5 weeks; haemoglobin recovers within minutes."},"answer":"B","why":"Fluid redistribution and renal retention can restore circulating volume over 12–72 hours. Erythropoietin-driven erythrocyte replacement takes roughly four to five weeks.","not":{"A":"New red-cell production cannot restore full mass within an hour.","C":"Volume replacement is generally faster than production of new erythrocytes.","D":"Restoring circulating fluid is quicker than replacing lost red-cell mass."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:d49eff66","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"In progressive shock, coronary perfusion falls sufficiently to impair the myocardium. Which vicious cycle follows?","options":{"A":"Weak contraction further lowers output and coronary flow.","B":"Increased cardiac output first causes the decline in coronary flow.","C":"Stronger contraction raises output and eliminates myocardial ischaemia.","D":"Reduced afterload restores all damaged myocardium without perfusion."},"answer":"A","why":"Poor coronary flow makes the heart ischaemic and weaker. Cardiac output and arterial pressure fall further, worsening myocardial perfusion in a self-amplifying loop.","not":{"B":"Reduced pumping and reduced coronary supply reinforce one another.","C":"Ischaemia depresses rather than enhances contraction.","D":"Lower afterload alone cannot reverse profound coronary and cellular injury."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:a260b2ec","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can prolonged cerebral ischaemia eventually reverse the early sympathetic compensation of shock?","options":{"A":"Carotid baroreceptors become more stretched as blood pressure declines.","B":"An increase in vagal withdrawal alone fully maintains blood pressure.","C":"Sustained oxygen deprivation depresses medullary vasomotor output.","D":"The vasomotor centre receives more oxygen and drives further constriction."},"answer":"C","why":"Prolonged inadequate brain perfusion can impair the vasomotor centre. Loss of sympathetic vascular support causes vasodilation, bradycardia and a further fall in perfusion.","not":{"A":"Declining pressure reduces rather than increases baroreceptor stretch.","B":"Loss of vasomotor output cannot be overcome by vagal withdrawal alone.","D":"Cerebral oxygen delivery falls, so this compensatory centre can eventually fail."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:03f825e8","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Late in progressive shock, precapillary vessels relax while postcapillary venules remain constricted. What is the likely consequence?","options":{"A":"Rapid capillary drainage with reduced pressure and plasma refill.","B":"Capillary stasis raises pressure and causes fluid loss.","C":"Complete restoration of tissue perfusion despite poor venous outflow.","D":"Lower capillary blood volume with immediate reversal of acidosis."},"answer":"B","why":"Blood enters capillaries but leaves poorly, creating stasis and an increase in capillary hydrostatic pressure. More fluid filters into tissues, further reducing vascular volume.","not":{"A":"Constriction of downstream venules restricts rather than accelerates drainage.","C":"Capillary stasis compounds, rather than resolves, impaired tissue perfusion.","D":"The described mismatch favours capillary pooling, not rapid decompression."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:0796d701","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"In the irreversible-shock flowchart, ATP depletion causes failure of cell sodium pumps. What cellular change follows?","options":{"A":"Potassium enters cells while sodium is pumped out faster.","B":"Plasma proteins rapidly replace lost intracellular ATP.","C":"Sodium and water leave cells, causing persistent shrinkage.","D":"Sodium and water enter cells, causing cellular swelling."},"answer":"D","why":"Cellular hypoxia leads to energy failure. Impaired sodium pumping allows sodium to accumulate inside cells, drawing water in and worsening cellular dysfunction, as depicted in slide 27.","not":{"A":"ATP depletion disrupts normal ion gradients; membrane pumps do not work faster.","B":"Protein movement cannot replace the energy required for membrane pumps.","C":"Impaired sodium pumping favours intracellular swelling rather than shrinkage."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:43d2f023","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"What makes irreversible shock different from a temporary shortage of circulating blood?","options":{"A":"Every type of irreversible shock is caused by an untreated arrhythmia.","B":"Vascular resistance increases indefinitely as cerebral function declines.","C":"Replacement of lost blood reliably reverses all advanced cell injury.","D":"Tissue injury may prevent recovery even after blood volume is replaced."},"answer":"D","why":"Irreversible shock occurs when sustained hypoperfusion causes damaging changes in heart, brain, vessels and cells. Restoring volume alone can no longer restore circulation or tissue function.","not":{"A":"Severe cell and organ injury can follow several causes, not only arrhythmia.","B":"Vasomotor failure may produce declining vascular resistance late in shock.","C":"Severe myocardial and cellular damage can persist despite volume restoration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:65a00cf4","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A patient with a femoral shaft fracture deteriorates from hypovolaemia despite no visible external blood loss. Which mechanism best explains this?","options":{"A":"The fracture necessarily causes acute left ventricular pump failure.","B":"Blood may collect internally within the injured thigh tissues.","C":"The fracture directly produces massive allergic vasodilation.","D":"External blood loss must be visible before hypovolaemia develops."},"answer":"B","why":"A fractured femur, ruptured spleen or ectopic pregnancy can cause concealed haemorrhage. Significant blood loss may occur into body spaces or tissues.","not":{"A":"A fracture does not by itself establish primary cardiogenic shock.","C":"Anaphylaxis requires a different trigger and vascular mechanism.","D":"Internal bleeding can cause hypovolaemia without external haemorrhage."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e0995212","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Which haematological pattern best distinguishes severe plasma loss from burns from the delayed effects of whole-blood haemorrhage?","options":{"A":"Burns may initially increase haematocrit through haemoconcentration.","B":"Burns immediately decrease haematocrit through selective red-cell loss.","C":"Burns leave plasma concentration unchanged despite leakage from skin.","D":"Burns increase haematocrit because erythropoietin acts instantly."},"answer":"A","why":"Burns allow plasma to escape from injured skin while more red cells remain intravascular, concentrating the blood. Whole-blood haemorrhage later causes dilution as fluid is replaced.","not":{"B":"Plasma rather than erythrocytes is the prominent lost component in burns.","C":"Loss of plasma changes the ratio of red cells to circulating fluid.","D":"Erythropoietin-mediated production takes weeks and cannot explain an immediate change."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e8426f18","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"After a crush injury, a patient in shock develops additional kidney damage. Which released substance best explains this injury?","options":{"A":"Haemoglobin synthesized rapidly by renal tubular cells.","B":"Albumin released from damaged capillary endothelium.","C":"Myoglobin liberated from injured skeletal muscle.","D":"Aldosterone released directly from necrotic muscle tissue."},"answer":"C","why":"Trauma can damage muscle, releasing myoglobin. Myoglobin can aggravate renal tubular injury in traumatic shock.","not":{"A":"Renal tubules do not synthesize haemoglobin as described.","B":"Albumin leak is not the specific nephrotoxic muscle product identified.","D":"Aldosterone is an adrenal hormone rather than a muscle breakdown product."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:76cf7c17","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"classification","stem":"A patient with acute circulatory collapse has cardiac tamponade. Which mechanical problem chiefly explains the shock category?","options":{"A":"Loss of plasma volume occurs through the skin into the interstitium.","B":"Primary myocardial cell death directly reduces contractile protein.","C":"External compression prevents normal ventricular filling.","D":"Massive vasodilation causes normal venous filling to become excessive."},"answer":"C","why":"Tamponade raises pressure around the heart and obstructs adequate filling. The resulting low output belongs to obstructive shock, unlike intrinsic pump failure or fluid loss.","not":{"A":"Plasma leak through burned skin is a hypovolaemic mechanism.","B":"Myocardial injury is a cardiogenic cause, not the mechanical restriction of filling.","D":"General vasodilation defines distributive shock rather than tamponade."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:c7bff26d","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"classification","stem":"A sudden large pulmonary embolus precipitates shock. What is the primary physiological obstacle?","options":{"A":"Obstruction prevents blood from reaching the lungs.","B":"Primary loss of fluid through renal sodium excretion.","C":"Systemic arteriolar dilation reduces total peripheral resistance.","D":"Direct suppression of red-cell synthesis within bone marrow."},"answer":"A","why":"A large pulmonary embolus physically obstructs blood flow through the pulmonary circulation, compromising forward flow and making this an example of obstructive shock.","not":{"B":"Renal fluid loss is not the immediate mechanism of embolic obstruction.","C":"Vasodilation describes a distributive rather than mechanical lesion.","D":"Reduced erythropoiesis cannot explain abrupt shock from an embolus."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:61a24fe7","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"clinical-application","stem":"After myocardial infarction, a patient develops poor perfusion with elevated systemic venous pressure. Which explanation best fits the combined observations?","options":{"A":"A primary capillary fluid leak immediately expands venous volume.","B":"Pump failure lowers output while blood backs up behind the heart.","C":"Widespread dilation causes low resistance with normal pump function.","D":"Whole-blood loss lowers central venous pressure without congestion."},"answer":"B","why":"Cardiogenic shock can combine low cardiac output with elevated filling and venous pressures because the diseased heart fails to move returning blood forward.","not":{"A":"Leaky capillaries reduce circulating volume rather than directly expand venous stores.","C":"A distributive mechanism is not required when intrinsic myocardial pumping has failed.","D":"Simple haemorrhage generally reduces filling and central venous pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5fed3099","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A patient in septic shock has widespread vasodilation and increased capillary permeability. How do these changes undermine circulation?","options":{"A":"They prevent blood from entering the venous system by raising stroke volume.","B":"They promote vascular pooling and loss of fluid into tissues.","C":"They increase total peripheral resistance and reduce filtration.","D":"They increase effective arterial filling and prevent tissue oedema."},"answer":"B","why":"Bacterial toxins can cause vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. The combination lowers effective circulating volume and resistance while promoting fluid escape to tissues.","not":{"A":"The main issue is loss of tone and barrier function, not increased stroke volume.","C":"Both vascular dilation and leakage work against those proposed pressure-defending effects.","D":"Fluid escape and vascular maldistribution impair rather than improve arterial filling."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:632ec87d","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Following an antigen-triggered systemic reaction, profound hypotension develops with widespread tissue swelling. Which mediator most directly explains the reaction?","options":{"A":"Aldosterone causing arterial dilation and large protein leakage.","B":"Erythropoietin causing acute venous pooling and capillary swelling.","C":"Histamine causes vasodilation and capillary leakage.","D":"Renin causing loss of all vascular smooth muscle responsiveness."},"answer":"C","why":"Anaphylaxis involves mediator release after immune activation. Histamine produces widespread dilation and increased capillary permeability, causing distributive shock and tissue fluid leakage.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone is chiefly associated with renal sodium retention, not this immediate syndrome.","B":"Erythropoietin has a delayed erythropoietic rather than acute vasoactive role.","D":"Renin is part of compensatory volume regulation rather than the trigger described."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1e778265","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"How may the pulse response of neurogenic shock differ from the typical early response in haemorrhagic shock?","options":{"A":"Neurogenic shock produces tachycardia solely from increased sympathetic drive.","B":"Neurogenic shock usually requires severe blood-volume depletion first.","C":"Neurogenic shock cannot involve a reduction in vascular resistance.","D":"Neurogenic loss of sympathetic tone may produce bradycardia."},"answer":"D","why":"Loss of sympathetic vascular and cardiac tone in neurogenic shock can cause vasodilation with a relatively slow heart rate. Haemorrhage commonly evokes reflex tachycardia.","not":{"A":"Sympathetic output is reduced rather than enhanced in this mechanism.","B":"A primary neural-tone failure can occur with preserved total blood volume.","C":"Low systemic resistance is a defining feature of this distributive state."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:9721fef7","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"classification","stem":"A patient has prolonged severe vomiting and diarrhoea followed by circulatory collapse. What is the most direct initial cause of the shock?","options":{"A":"Loss of extracellular fluid reduces effective circulating volume.","B":"Acute myocardial infarction is required for the low cardiac output.","C":"Sepsis-related vasodilation must precede any gastrointestinal fluid loss.","D":"A pulmonary arterial obstruction prevents right ventricular output."},"answer":"A","why":"Diarrhoea and vomiting can cause hypovolaemia through fluid loss. Dehydration lowers circulating volume and venous return, reducing cardiac filling and tissue perfusion.","not":{"B":"Pump failure is not required when the main problem is volume depletion.","C":"Gastrointestinal losses can cause hypovolaemia without sepsis.","D":"A mechanical pulmonary obstruction is a different mechanism."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e508622d","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A man becomes light-headed and faints while urinating at night, several minutes after rising from bed. What most specifically identifies the type of event?","options":{"A":"A carotid-sinus episode triggered by pressure at the neck.","B":"An obstructive circulatory collapse from pulmonary embolism.","C":"A primary pump-failure episode caused by severe arrhythmia.","D":"A situational syncopal episode associated with voiding."},"answer":"D","why":"Fainting specifically during urination fits micturition syncope, a situational cause of transient inadequate cerebral perfusion. Timing during voiding distinguishes it from a faint explained solely by posture.","not":{"A":"Carotid sinus syncope follows compression of a sensitive neck region, not an episode linked to voiding.","B":"Obstructive shock from an embolus would not be identified by a specific link to urination.","C":"Arrhythmia can cause syncope, but the stem gives no supporting evidence for a cardiac rhythm disturbance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:891a2b31","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient faints after pressure from a tight collar over a sensitive carotid sinus. What is the physiological trigger?","options":{"A":"Carotid sinus stretch triggers an exaggerated depressor reflex.","B":"Carotid body hypoxia causes isolated tachycardia and vasoconstriction.","C":"Peripheral muscle ischemia releases a reflex that raises heart rate.","D":"Pulmonary venous stretch directly produces aortic obstruction."},"answer":"A","why":"A tight collar can stimulate a sensitive carotid sinus and trigger syncope. Extra sinus stretch can activate reflex bradycardia and vasodilation, reducing cerebral perfusion.","not":{"B":"Carotid bodies are chemoreceptors, not the stretched sinus receptors described.","C":"Raising heart rate would usually oppose the fainting mechanism.","D":"Pulmonary stretch cannot explain a neck-pressure-triggered episode."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b4aa9ae3","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"1-what-is-heart-failure","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"A patient has symptoms of heart failure despite near-normal resting cardiac output. Which additional finding best makes heart failure physiologically plausible?","options":{"A":"The output is high because the kidneys excrete excess sodium.","B":"The output is normal with no evidence of any upstream congestion.","C":"The output is maintained with low venous and atrial pressures.","D":"Adequate output requires unusually high filling pressure."},"answer":"D","why":"Heart failure can mean that adequate output is achieved only at excessive filling pressure. Such elevated pressure causes congestion even when resting output appears near normal.","not":{"A":"Renal sodium excretion is not the proposed compensatory failure mechanism.","B":"The stem needs another sign of dysfunction, which high filling pressure supplies.","C":"Normal output at normal filling pressure does not support this pump-failure explanation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:bf7054b3","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"1-what-is-heart-failure","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which pair best distinguishes forward and backward consequences of a failing ventricular pump?","options":{"A":"Forward failure congests veins; backward failure increases systemic output.","B":"Forward failure raises capillary filtration; backward failure lowers venous pressure.","C":"Forward failure limits tissue flow; backward failure congests upstream veins.","D":"Forward failure protects organ perfusion; backward failure reduces tissue oxygen use."},"answer":"C","why":"Insufficient forward output deprives downstream tissues of blood. Backward failure raises pressure in the venous circulation feeding the failing chamber, causing fluid congestion.","not":{"A":"These directions confuse inadequate ejection with upstream pressure build-up.","B":"Backward failure raises, rather than lowers, upstream venous pressure.","D":"Both consequences are harmful rather than automatically protective."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:9ecaf235","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"mechanism","stem":"An injured left ventricle contracts weakly and ejects a smaller proportion of its end-diastolic volume. What type of dysfunction is most consistent?","options":{"A":"Normal ventricular relaxation with improved stroke volume.","B":"Systolic failure with reduced ejection fraction.","C":"Diastolic failure with preserved ejection fraction.","D":"Isolated right atrial dysfunction with normal left output."},"answer":"B","why":"Poor contractility reduces the fraction of filled volume ejected with each beat, which is characteristic of systolic ventricular dysfunction.","not":{"A":"Weak contraction makes improved stroke volume unlikely.","C":"Diastolic dysfunction is primarily impaired relaxation and filling rather than weak ejection.","D":"The problem is explicitly in left ventricular contraction, not isolated atrial function."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:0f401f8d","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"A hypertrophied ventricle is stiff, has high filling pressure, but ejects a near-normal fraction of the blood it receives. Which interpretation is best?","options":{"A":"A preserved ejection fraction excludes symptomatic heart failure.","B":"Diastolic failure with preserved ejection fraction.","C":"High filling pressure proves that ventricular compliance has increased.","D":"Systolic failure is required whenever myocardial wall thickness increases."},"answer":"B","why":"A stiff ventricle resists filling, so high pressure may be necessary to deliver even a modest end-diastolic volume. Ejection fraction can remain preserved despite congestion.","not":{"A":"Ejection fraction does not establish normal ventricular filling or exclude pump failure.","C":"Reduced compliance, not increased compliance, is the meaning of a stiff ventricle.","D":"Wall thickening alone does not require reduced systolic ejection."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:cd24848f","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why may a marked rise in heart rate particularly worsen filling in a stiff ventricle?","options":{"A":"It lowers atrial pressure even if the ventricle cannot relax.","B":"It lengthens diastole, promoting excess ventricular relaxation.","C":"It shortens diastole, limiting ventricular inflow.","D":"It directly increases ventricular compliance without changing preload."},"answer":"C","why":"Tachycardia shortens diastolic time. A stiff ventricle already fills with difficulty, so reduced filling time can further lower ventricular filling and raise pressure.","not":{"A":"A poorly relaxing ventricle may require high atrial pressure, not lower pressure.","B":"Higher heart rate usually shortens, not lengthens, the diastolic interval.","D":"Tachycardia does not immediately remove myocardial stiffness."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:9517b372","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with left ventricular failure develops lung crackles and breathlessness. Which sequence best explains pulmonary fluid accumulation?","options":{"A":"Systemic venous constriction directly lowers pulmonary interstitial fluid.","B":"Right atrial pressure falls, reducing pulmonary capillary filtration.","C":"Pulmonary venous pressure falls, increasing plasma oncotic pressure.","D":"Pulmonary venous pressure rises and drives fluid filtration."},"answer":"D","why":"Left ventricular failure raises pressure upstream in the left atrium and lung veins. Pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure increases, driving fluid into lung tissue and impairing gas exchange.","not":{"A":"The direct mechanism is pulmonary venous back-pressure, not systemic venous constriction alone.","B":"Lower pulmonary capillary pressure would oppose rather than cause congestion.","C":"Reduced venous pressure does not drive the described oedema formation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:713af4f1","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"application","stem":"A breathless patient with left-sided failure sleeps propped up and deteriorates on lying flat. What change during recumbency makes breathing worse?","options":{"A":"More peripheral venous blood returns to the chest and raises lung pressure.","B":"Pulmonary capillary pressure falls while interstitial fluid rises.","C":"The heart immediately develops a higher ejection fraction when supine.","D":"Less peripheral venous blood returns to the heart and empties the lung veins."},"answer":"A","why":"Lying flat mobilises blood stored in dependent vessels to the thorax. A failing left ventricle cannot handle the extra return, so pulmonary venous pressure and congestion worsen.","not":{"B":"Pulmonary oedema here is associated with increased, not decreased, capillary pressure.","C":"An immediate improvement in ejection fraction would not cause this worsening.","D":"Reduced venous return would not explain the additional pulmonary congestion."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e0c0a990","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A patient has raised jugular venous pressure, tender hepatomegaly, ascites and ankle oedema. Which haemodynamic disturbance best unifies these findings?","options":{"A":"Right ventricular failure raises pressure throughout systemic veins.","B":"Left ventricular failure selectively reduces pulmonary venous pressure.","C":"Low plasma volume directly increases jugular venous drainage.","D":"Aortic valve narrowing selectively raises right atrial compliance."},"answer":"A","why":"When the right ventricle cannot move returning blood forward, systemic venous and right atrial pressures rise. Congestion then affects neck veins, liver and dependent tissues.","not":{"B":"Left failure characteristically elevates pulmonary rather than isolated systemic venous pressure.","C":"Low circulating volume usually lowers venous distension, not raises JVP.","D":"Changes in aortic valve area do not directly produce this full venous-congestion pattern."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6d1d8c74","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"mechanism","stem":"Long-standing left-sided failure is followed by right ventricular dysfunction. Which link most directly connects them?","options":{"A":"Left ventricular pressure empties the right ventricle directly.","B":"Pulmonary congestion raises right ventricular pumping load.","C":"Systemic venous pressure immediately falls to nearly zero.","D":"Renal sodium excretion reduces the volume entering the right heart."},"answer":"B","why":"Persistent left-sided back-pressure can raise pressure within the pulmonary circulation. The right ventricle must pump against this greater load and may eventually fail.","not":{"A":"The ventricles pump through two linked circulations, not directly into each other.","C":"Loss of systemic venous pressure is not the proposed cause of right failure.","D":"Sodium retention, rather than enhanced excretion, is typical of the associated compensation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:8a285e11","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A failing right ventricle has severely reduced output despite blood arriving from the systemic veins. What additional forward-flow effect is expected?","options":{"A":"More right ventricular output directly produces systemic oedema.","B":"More pulmonary venous flow immediately raises left ventricular preload.","C":"Less systemic venous pressure improves left ventricular filling.","D":"Less pulmonary flow means less reaches the left heart."},"answer":"D","why":"Forward failure of the right ventricle reduces pulmonary flow. Less blood then reaches the left atrium, limiting left ventricular filling and potentially systemic output.","not":{"A":"The stem specifies reduced, not increased, right ventricular ejection.","B":"Poor right ventricular output decreases rather than boosts pulmonary venous return.","C":"Systemic venous congestion may rise while downstream left filling is limited."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:d5758508","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"clinical-application","stem":"Fatigue, poor exercise tolerance and oliguria predominate in a patient with failing left ventricular output. What mechanism best explains these symptoms?","options":{"A":"Excess forward flow produces hyperperfusion of skeletal muscle and kidneys.","B":"Isolated pulmonary venous return falls without any change in tissue delivery.","C":"Selective venous pooling in the head improves systemic oxygen transport.","D":"Inadequate forward flow reduces skeletal-muscle and renal perfusion."},"answer":"D","why":"Forward left-sided failure limits arterial delivery to organs. Reduced muscle perfusion contributes to fatigue, while reduced renal perfusion and neurohormonal activation promote low urine output.","not":{"A":"Improved peripheral delivery would not account for the symptoms specified.","B":"Forward left-sided failure involves inadequate systemic tissue perfusion.","C":"Cranial venous pooling would not restore arterial flow to muscle and kidney."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:3c5e885a","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with heart failure is oedematous but continues to retain sodium and water. What do the kidneys primarily respond to?","options":{"A":"High effective arterial perfusion despite reduced total body fluid.","B":"Poor arterial filling despite excess total body fluid.","C":"A direct measurement of swollen ankle volume by renal tubules.","D":"Increased atrial filling that completely suppresses renin release."},"answer":"B","why":"The failing pump inadequately fills the arterial circulation even while veins and tissues are congested. Renal hypoperfusion activates salt- and water-retaining mechanisms.","not":{"A":"The body has excess total fluid but the arterial side can remain underperfused.","C":"Renal sensing is driven by perfusion and hormonal signals, not direct ankle volume measurement.","D":"Renin and other retention pathways may remain active despite systemic congestion."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:944e6887","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"causal-sequence","stem":"Why may sustained renin–angiotensin–aldosterone activation eventually worsen heart failure?","options":{"A":"More afterload and salt retention promote congestion.","B":"Vasodilation lowers afterload while sodium excretion relieves oedema.","C":"Lower circulating volume reduces venous return and reverses all congestion.","D":"Reduced arterial resistance eliminates myocardial work in the failing heart."},"answer":"A","why":"Angiotensin II increases vascular resistance, while aldosterone promotes sodium and water retention. These help preserve pressure short term but load the failing ventricle and worsen fluid overload.","not":{"B":"The RAAS responses here favour constriction and retention, not dilation and natriuresis.","C":"RAAS expands rather than depletes volume in this setting.","D":"Increased afterload means the ventricle must work against greater resistance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:3c59f3bb","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can a sustained sympathetic rise in heart failure eventually reduce cardiac performance?","options":{"A":"Tachycardia lengthens filling while arteriolar dilation raises afterload.","B":"Vasodilation and slower rates consistently worsen ventricular workload.","C":"Faster heart rate shortens filling; arteriolar tone raises afterload.","D":"Vagal stimulation increases heart rate and reduces vascular resistance."},"answer":"C","why":"Sympathetic activity temporarily supports rate and pressure. Persistently rapid rate reduces diastolic filling time, while arteriolar constriction increases the load against which the heart ejects.","not":{"A":"Faster rate reduces diastolic time, and vasoconstriction rather than dilation raises afterload.","B":"This option reverses the actual long-term sympathetic changes described.","D":"Vagal stimulation usually slows rather than accelerates the heart."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:81e44e24","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"A failing heart operates on a flattened Frank–Starling curve. What happens when additional blood volume raises ventricular end-diastolic pressure?","options":{"A":"Stroke volume rises steeply while filling pressure stays unchanged.","B":"Stroke volume increases proportionately to every added millilitre.","C":"Stroke volume rises little while filling pressure and congestion increase.","D":"Ventricular performance returns to normal without change in contractility."},"answer":"C","why":"On the flat portion of the failing Starling relation, extra preload gives little extra output. It mainly elevates filling pressures and worsens venous and tissue congestion.","not":{"A":"A steep output rise characterises a more responsive part of the relation.","B":"The failing ventricle does not convert every preload increase into proportional ejection.","D":"Added volume alone cannot necessarily repair impaired myocardial pump function."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:fd5424d4","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"In chronic heart failure, vasopressin remains elevated. Which paired effect is most consistent with its action and the clinical problem?","options":{"A":"Increased renal water retention may aggravate fluid overload.","B":"Reduced plasma volume directly restores cardiac contractility.","C":"Increased renal water excretion relieves venous congestion.","D":"Rapid production of new red cells eliminates pulmonary oedema."},"answer":"A","why":"Vasopressin promotes renal water reabsorption. Although this can defend pressure initially, sustained retention adds fluid to a congested circulation.","not":{"B":"It does not directly restore failing myocardial contractility by lowering plasma volume.","C":"Vasopressin is antidiuretic, so it generally reduces rather than increases water excretion.","D":"Red-cell production is unrelated to the main antidiuretic effect."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:359688cf","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"long-term-mechanism","stem":"Over months, a ventricle exposed to chronic pressure and neurohormonal stimulation changes its shape and size. Why can this be harmful?","options":{"A":"Chamber dilation prevents activation of any compensatory hormones.","B":"Remodelling can impair myocardial pumping efficiency.","C":"Remodelling guarantees normal output independently of filling pressure.","D":"Wall thickening permanently removes all effects of increased afterload."},"answer":"B","why":"Over time, ventricular dilation or thickening can compromise pumping efficiency and perpetuate the heart-failure cycle.","not":{"A":"A dilated chamber does not automatically switch off maladaptive neurohormonal activation.","C":"Structural change is not guaranteed to normalise function.","D":"Hypertrophy may compensate initially but does not erase sustained loading effects."}}]};
